DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The RIPEMD160_ functions calculate a 160-bit cryptographic checksum (di‐
     gest) for any number of input bytes.  A cryptographic checksum is a one-
     way hash function; that is, it is computationally impractical to find the
     input corresponding to a particular output.  This net result is a
     “fingerprint” of the input-data, which does not disclose the actual in‐
     put.

     The RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__IInniitt(), RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__UUppddaattee(), and RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__FFiinnaall() functions
     are the core functions.  Allocate an _R_I_P_E_M_D_1_6_0___C_T_X, initialize it with
     RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__IInniitt(), run over the data with RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__UUppddaattee(), and finally
     extract the result using RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__FFiinnaall(), which will also erase the
     _R_I_P_E_M_D_1_6_0___C_T_X.

     The RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__EEnndd() function is a wrapper for RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__FFiinnaall() which
     converts the return value to a 41-character (including the terminating
     '\0') ASCII string which represents the 160 bits in hexadecimal.

     The RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__FFiillee() function calculates the digest of a file, and uses
     RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__EEnndd() to return the result.  If the file cannot be opened, a
     null pointer is returned.  The RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() function is similar
     to RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__FFiillee(), but it only calculates the digest over a byte-range
     of the file specified, starting at _o_f_f_s_e_t and spanning _l_e_n_g_t_h bytes.  If
     the _l_e_n_g_t_h parameter is specified as 0, or more than the length of the
     remaining part of the file, RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__FFiilleeCChhuunnkk() calculates the digest
     from _o_f_f_s_e_t to the end of file.  The RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__DDaattaa() function calculates
     the digest of a chunk of data in memory, and uses RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__EEnndd() to re‐
     turn the result.

     When using RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__EEnndd(), RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__FFiillee(), or RRIIPPEEMMDD116600__DDaattaa(), the
     _b_u_f argument can be a null pointer, in which case the returned string is
     allocated with malloc(3) and subsequently must be explicitly deallocated
     using free(3) after use.  If the _b_u_f argument is non-null it must point
     to at least 41 characters of buffer space.

